---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Method"
  subtitle="What the labels mean, and who is missing"
  lede="Every claim here carries a confidence and a source. The point of labelling is that a reader can overturn the reasoning without having to take the conclusion on trust.">

  <h2>The labels</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="tag doc">Documented</span></td><td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan. The Gedenkbuch entries, the marriage certificate number, the deportation dates.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></td><td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be attacked. The paternity finding is here, and stays here until a Y-DNA result moves it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag disp">Disputed</span></td><td>Asserted in the family record and contradicted by what can be seen. Georg Leibholz as a brother; the February 1948 marriage date.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag disp">Unproven</span></td><td>Kept because it may matter later, but carrying no weight now. The hundred-odd surname-index Luwinskis.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      <strong>Living people are omitted from this build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not
      present in the output. <strong>{register.counts.omitted_living}</strong> people were removed on
      that ground before this site was generated.
    </p>
    <p>
      One exception, and only one. <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a> is
      <strong>named</strong>, with his birth year and his parents, and nothing else — no occupation, no
      address, no photograph, no descendants. An archive about a man's ancestry that would not say whose
      ancestry it was would be useless; an archive that published him would be a different kind of
      failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the source flags someone as living but they were born in 1920 or earlier, and so would now
      be 106 or more, they are presumed dead and published with the absence of a death record stated
      plainly. Several of those are Holocaust-era people whose fate was never recorded. Saying so is the
      entry's purpose.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Two rules for this particular family</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>On the Holocaust records.</strong> Dates and places come from the Bundesarchiv and are
    quoted as the Bundesarchiv gives them, including its refusals: where it declines to state a date of
    death because the date is not certain, this archive declines too, and says why. Jacob Leibholz has
    no death date here. That is not an omission.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>On the paternity.</strong> It is stated, because burying a documented inconsistency in a
    private file would make the rest of the archive worth less. It is labelled as an inference, the
    arithmetic is shown, and the tests that would overturn it are listed. DNA match holders are living
    people and are not named.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections to the source</h2>
  <p>
    Where a named source overrides the family record, the correction is applied to the register and
    listed here with the evidence. <strong>Nothing is silently rewritten</strong> — the original
    value is carried through as <em>was</em>, so a reader can always see what the family believed and
    why this archive disagrees.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Field</th><th>Was</th><th>Now</th><th>On what evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{register.corrections.map(c => (
      <tr><td><strong>{c.who}</strong></td><td>{c.field}</td><td class="muted">{c.was}</td><td><strong>{c.now}</strong></td><td class="small">{c.source}</td></tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Duplicate records in the source</h2>
  <p>
    The tree holds five people twice. Each pair shares an identical birth <em>and</em> death date, so
    these are not two people with one name — they are one person entered twice, usually once with
    parents attached and once without. They are collapsed here to the fuller copy, and listed so the
    collapse can be checked or reversed.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Dropped</th><th>Kept</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{register.duplicates.map(d => (<tr><td>{d.name}</td><td class="small muted">{d.dropped}</td><td class="small">{d.kept}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small muted">
    One of these mattered. The dateless copy of <strong>Leopold Leibke</strong> was the only record in
    the whole tree flagged as a living person without a birth year — while the fuller copy of the same
    man records his death in 1932. The build's living-persons check caught it, which is what the check
    is for.
  </p>

  <h2>The source</h2>
  <p>
    The spine is a MyHeritage family tree of 164 people, read on 14 September 2026. It is a family
    record, not an archive: it carries real documents, real citations, family estimates and at least two
    duplicated individuals, all mixed together at the same apparent confidence. Separating those is most
    of the work here. Where this archive contradicts the tree — Jacob's death year, Georg's parentage,
    the February 1948 marriage — the contradiction is set out with its evidence rather than quietly
    corrected.
  </p>
</Base>
